<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.1.1: Understanding Exponents</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01227</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.1.1: Understanding Exponents</md:title>
    <md:uuid>8ff190b6-c37b-417b-861f-94abb21a64f5</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up (5 minutes)</title>

<para>This warm up reviews and reinforces the idea that exponents can be represented equivalently as repeated multiplication. Students will translate constant and variable bases with exponents to multiplication expressions.</para>
<title> Launch</title>
<para>Display the introduction and the example expressions. Ask the class to explain the relationship between exponents and repeated multiplication. Display the exponential expressions. Give students quiet think-time for each problem and ask them to give a signal when they have an answer. Keep all problems displayed throughout the talk. Follow with a whole-class discussion as needed to review the answers.</para>

<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>An exponent indicates repeated multiplication of the same quantity.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="First example: a raised to the power of m equals a times a times a times a and so on until you have multiplied m different factors of a together. Second example: the quantity negative 9 raised to the power of 5 equals negative 9 times negative 9 times negative 9 times negative 9 times negative 9, a total of 5 factors of negative 9."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/52807ddc47e36cc0c088995b28577d0de4058c58"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Expand each exponential expression to show repeated multiplication.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(4^3\) = ____________ </item>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(4 \cdot 4 \cdot 4\)</para>
  <item> \(x^5\) = ____________ </item>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(x \cdot x \cdot x \cdot x \cdot x\)</para>
  <item>\(
    (-8)^6\) = ____________ </item>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((-8) \cdot (-8) \cdot (-8) \cdot (-8) \cdot (-8) \cdot (-8)\)</para>
  <item>\((-z)^3\) = ____________ </item>
  <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((-z) \cdot (-z) \cdot (-z)\)</para>
</list>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Invite students to share their answers as each expression is simplified into repeated multiplication. Then, discuss with students:</para>
<list>
  <item> “What is the relationship between the base of the exponent and the factor used in the repeated multiplication expression?” (The base of the exponent is the same value or variable that is used in the repeated multiplication expression.) </item>
  <item> “What is the relationship between the exponent value and the number of factors in the repeated multiplication expression?” (The exponent value will be equal to the number of factors in the repeated multiplication expression.) </item>
</list></section>
